{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Take from among you an offering to HaShem.</b> We have already explained above that 'from among you' comes to exclude stolen property. And 'from among you' may further include that each person should bestir himself to the commandment on his own initiative — for there is a person who gives nothing at all until he sees others giving, and he is stirred to jealousy by them; therefore he gives to make himself equal to them. But this is not 'from among you,' for he himself is not the cause of the giving; rather, others are the cause for him. And some say 'from among you' quite literally: for one who gives from the willingness of his heart is as though he had offered his very body, and furthermore the poor person who gives is as though he takes from his own flesh and gives, in the manner of what is said: ״וּמִבְּשָׂרְךָ אַל תִּתְעַלָּם״ (Isaiah 58:7) — do not hide yourself from taking even from your own flesh and giving." "<b>Everyone whose heart is willing shall bring it.</b> First it says ״קְחוּ מֵאִתְּכֶם״, and the language of taking implies that they shall take from him against his will; and afterward it says ״כֹּל נְדִיב לֵב יְבִיאֶהָ״, which implies that he shall bring of his own accord. And likewise in parashat Terumah the language of taking is also mentioned. Furthermore, here it says ״כֹּל נְדִיב לֵב״, while there (Exodus 25:2) it says ״מֵאֵת כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יִדְּבֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ״, and it should have said יִנְדְּבֶנּוּ. Rather, it is speaking of two kinds of giving: for there it speaks of two offerings that are obligatory, and he is required to give even if his heart is not willing — and on the contrary, his heart languishes over him and worries and aches because of the giving; nonetheless you shall take from him by force. Therefore it says ״מֵאֵת כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יִדְּבֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ״ — as if it said 'יִדְּוֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ', for bet interchanges with vav among the letters בּוּמַ״ף, and particularly a soft bet. And it means: whoever's heart languishes over him and does not give his obligation willingly — you shall take his offering from him by force. And likewise what it says, ״קְחוּ מֵאִתְּכֶם תְּרוּמָה לַה׳״, also speaks of an obligatory offering, namely the half-shekel per head. And perhaps it is for this reason that it says 'מֵאִתְּכֶם' — from your own heads (גֻּלְגֹּלֶת). And afterward it speaks of an unconstrained gift: every willing heart shall bring it of his own accord, without compulsion — for the willing heart is the opposite of him whose heart languishes over him (יִדְּוֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ), and he brings his gift of himself; he does not wait until the collector comes to demand from him what he has already pledged. And one may explain further, that here it says ״נְדִיב לֵב״ and there it says ״אֲשֶׁר יִדְּבֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ״, and it should have said יִנְדְּבֶנּוּ. Rather, there it speaks of two obligatory offerings, and there the language of taking is used because it must be taken even by force; but here, where it speaks of a free-will gift, the language of bringing is used, for the willing heart brings of himself.\n\nAnd one may explain further: here it says ״נְדִיב לֵב״ and there it says ״יִדְּבֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ״ — יִדְּבֶנּוּ being like יִדְּוֶנּוּ, for bet interchanges with vav among the letters בּוּמַ״ף, and particularly a soft bet. And there, where it speaks of obligatory offerings, it uses the expression of יִדְּבֶנּוּ — his heart languishes (דּוֶֹה) over him, that is to say, it aches over the giving; and nonetheless it says ״תִּקְחוּ״, that it must be taken from him by force. But here, where it speaks of a free-will offering, it says ״כֹּל נְדִיב לֵב״, and such a person is the opposite of one whose heart aches — for his heart is generous (נָדִיב) and he brings of himself, as it says ״יְבִיאֶהָ״.\n\nAnd perhaps 'מֵאִתְּכֶם' means from your heads — that is, the half-shekel per head, which is an obligatory offering concerning which it says ״קְחוּ״, take by force; and afterward it speaks of a free-will offering: ״כֹּל נְדִיב לֵב יְבִיאֶהָ״, of his own accord, without compulsion, for the willing heart is the opposite of one whose heart aches, and he brings his gift of himself, not waiting until the collector comes to demand of him what he pledged." "And it further appears to me, that first it says ״מֵאִתְּכֶם תְּרוּמָה״, and afterward it says ״אֵת תְּרוּמַת ה׳״. This is according to the mindset of the giver: for one who does not give willingly does not consider that he is giving from his own property at all, but rather from the property of the Holy One, blessed be He — for to Him, blessed be He, belong silver and gold; rather, he errs in his mind and thinks that his own strength and the might of his hand made him this wealth. Therefore it says of him: ״קְחוּ מֵאִתְּכֶם תְּרוּמָה לַה׳״ — meaning, from those factions who think that you are taking from what is theirs, for HaShem. But 'כֹּל נְדִיב לֵב' — one who knows that to Him, blessed be He, belongs the silver, and that he is giving from what is His — therefore ״יְבִיאֶהָ״ of himself, ״אֵת תְּרוּמַת ה׳״, meaning that it already belongs to HaShem. That is why it is called here 'תְּרוּמַת ה׳', from which it may be understood that it already belongs to HaShem. And therefore it says ״וְהֵם הֵבִיאוּ אֵלָיו עוֹד נְדָבָה בַּבֹּקֶר בַּבֹּקֶר״ (Exodus 36:3) — what is 'עוֹד'? Rather, it says that two good signs appeared in them, and they are miraculous proof that they gave their gift with joy: first, that they brought of themselves before the collector came to them to demand what each had already pledged; second, that they brought in the morning with a bright countenance — for everything given with a bright countenance is given in the morning, and the manna is proof of this, as Rashi explained in parashat Beshalah (Exodus 16:7). And 'בַּבֹּקֶר' further alludes that each one of them who was able to get ahead of his fellow did get ahead — therefore 'בַּבֹּקֶר' is stated twice: once, corresponding to the fact that they gave with a bright countenance; and second, corresponding to the fact that the zealous ones would get ahead of others."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {1 "The comment contains overlapping restatements of the same arguments (the bet/vav interchange, the two types of giving, the interpretation of מאתכם from the head). The Hebrew itself appears to repeat the same derashot with slight variation, possibly reflecting oral delivery or textual layering; all repetitions are rendered faithfully in order."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:49.102546937Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}